course: dictionary
lesson: 7672
title: Proportion: parts, sizes and fair shares
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Proportion is a word about parts and wholes. A proportion is a part of a whole, thought of as a share: «a large proportion of the class walks to school» means lots of the class, but not all of it.
explain: Proportion can also mean how big one thing is compared with another. «Proportion means how big each part is compared with the others.» So when you meet this word, ask yourself: compared with what?
explain: Things are in proportion when every part is the right size compared with the others. Things are out of proportion when one part is too big or too small. Feelings can be out of proportion too, when they are blown up more than the event deserves.
explain: Proportions, with an s, can mean the size and shape of something: «a room of huge proportions» is a very big room. Maths uses the word too, and you will meet it in fractions, percentages and maps.
fact: A proportion is a part of a whole, thought of as a share.
fact: Proportion means how big one thing is compared with another.
fact: In maths, a proportion says that two ratios are equal, like 1 : 2 = 3 : 6.
fact: «For most people the arm span from fingertip to fingertip is about the same as their height», and Leonardo da Vinci drew a famous man in a circle and a square to show such proportions.
fact: Phrases to learn: in proportion to, a proportion of, direct proportion, out of proportion, sense of proportion.
exercise: In «a large proportion of the class walks to school», what does proportion mean? | The children's ages | *A share, a part of the whole class | A rule about lunch | A loud noise
exercise: If two amounts are in proportion, what happens when you double one of them? | *The other doubles too | The other is halved | The other stays the same | The other disappears
exercise: What does «a room of huge proportions» tell you? | A room with no windows | A room that smells nice | *A room that is very big | A room that is very cold
test: In maths, 1 : 2 = 3 : 6 is an example of what? | *A proportion | A fraction of a pizza | A type of shape | A rule for spelling
test: Sam cries for an hour because he lost one small game. What does this show? | Sam is very tall for his age | Sam is in proportion | *Sam's feelings are out of proportion | Sam has won a prize
exam: In the sentence about Leonardo da Vinci, what did he draw to show? | A new kind of car | A recipe for pizza | A map of Italy | *Proportions of the human body
